# Explainable and Cross-Project Code Smell Detection Using Software Metrics

Research-grade static analysis and machine learning platform that detects code
smells, explains every prediction, and — the part that makes it research —
measures how well those predictions transfer to projects the model has never
seen.

**Current status: M0-M9 complete** (secure ingestion, metrics, rule baseline,
blinded datasets, leakage-safe cross-project ML, persistent FastAPI jobs, trusted
model serving, SHAP explanations, React dashboard, role-based authentication,
versioned reports, research exports and production deployment). Python-first.

---

## What works today

```bash
pip install -e ".[dev]"

codesmell ingest ./my-project          # a directory
codesmell ingest ./project.zip         # an uploaded archive
codesmell ingest ./module.py           # a single file
codesmell ingest https://github.com/psf/requests   # a public repository

codesmell ingest ./my-project --json --rejections
codesmell config                       # effective settings and wired plugins

codesmell analyze ./my-project                       # 32 metrics per class
codesmell analyze ./my-project --type method \
    --sort cognitive_complexity --limit 20           # 33 metrics per method
codesmell analyze https://github.com/psf/requests --sort wmc

codesmell detect ./my-project                        # rule-based baseline
codesmell detect ./my-project --explain              # condition trail per finding
codesmell detect ./my-project --thresholds percentile   # project-relative
codesmell detect ./my-project --json | jq            # baseline evidence

# M4: create a blinded human-review bundle from one or more projects
codesmell dataset create ./project-a ./project-b -o dataset_m4
codesmell dataset validate dataset_m4/review_tasks.csv
codesmell dataset validate dataset_m4/review_tasks.csv --require-complete
codesmell dataset finalize dataset_m4/review_tasks.csv -o labels.csv
codesmell dataset agreement reviewer_a.csv reviewer_b.csv \
    --conflicts conflicts.csv

# M5: leakage-safe machine learning
codesmell ml prepare labels.csv ./project-a ./project-b -o training_features.csv
codesmell ml train training_features.csv -o models_m5
codesmell ml logo training_features.csv -o logo_m5

# M6-M9: database, API, trusted models, authentication and persistent worker
codesmell db upgrade
codesmell model register models_m5/long_method/logistic --name "Long Method LR"
codesmell model list
codesmell api serve --reload       # terminal 1
codesmell worker run              # terminal 2

# M9: users, reports and publication exports
codesmell user create admin@example.org --name "Administrator" --role admin
codesmell report generate <analysis-job-id> --format pdf
codesmell research summarize models_m5/holdout_report.json \
    logo_m5/logo_report.json -o research_m9
```

`ingest` produces a `ProjectInventory`: source files, per-language counts,
primary language, build tools, declared dependencies, everything refused and
why, and a content fingerprint.

`analyze` runs the two-pass metrics engine over it and ranks entities by any
extracted metric.

### Metrics extracted

**Every entity** — LOC, SLOC, comment lines, blank lines, comment density,
docstring presence, the full Halstead suite (vocabulary, length, volume,
difficulty, effort, time, estimated bugs), maintainability index.

**Classes (32 features)** — WMC, DIT, NOC, CBO, RFC, LCOM1, LCOM\*
(Henderson-Sellers), fan-in, fan-out, method and field counts, public method
count, average method complexity, base class count.

**Methods and functions (33 features)** — cyclomatic complexity, cognitive
complexity, max nesting depth, parameter counts, local variable count, return
count, call and distinct-call counts, longest message chain, magic number
count, foreign access ratio (the Feature Envy signal), accessed field count,
decorator count.

Class and method feature spaces are deliberately disjoint: LCOM is undefined
for a method and nesting depth is undefined for a class. `FeatureSchema`
enforces this and pins a stable column order, so a model trained today cannot
misread a matrix built tomorrow.

### Rule-based detection

13 smells detected from YAML thresholds, no ML involved. Every threshold cites
its source; values without a citation are marked `heuristic`, because the
distinction matters when the numbers appear in a paper.

Two threshold modes. **Absolute** uses published values (Lanza & Marinescu,
McCabe, Fowler, Campbell). **Percentile** recomputes each threshold from the
analysed project's own distribution.

Running both on `psf/requests` produced a result worth recording: percentile
mode gave 468 findings against absolute mode's 253, and the excess sat almost
entirely in single-condition rules — `message_chain` went from 0 to 177,
because the 95th percentile of chain length in that codebase is 2. **A
percentile threshold flags a fixed share of entities by construction**, healthy
project or not. Multi-condition rules resist the effect (God Class returns 6 in
both modes) because a conjunction cannot be satisfied by distribution position
alone.

That is a limitation of project-relative *thresholding*, not of project-relative
*framing* — and it sharpens the M5 plan: the percentile view belongs in feature
scaling, where a model learns what a distribution position implies, not in
thresholding, where it fixes the positive rate in advance.

Rules carry **structural exemptions**, and they are not optional. The first run
of these detectors on this project's own source produced 143 findings, 82 of
which were exception subclasses and enums flagged as Lazy Class or Data Class.
Those classes are *supposed* to look that way — it is a false positive by
construction, not a threshold that needs tuning. With exemptions the same run
produces 47 findings.

## M4 human-labelling dataset workflow

`codesmell dataset create` accepts one or more directories, ZIP archives,
single Python files, or public HTTPS repositories. It analyses every project,
uses M3 only to propose likely positives, samples same-entity-type controls,
and writes a reproducible review bundle:

```text
dataset_m4/
├── review_tasks.csv          # blinded reviewer sheet; no metrics or rule verdict
├── candidate_evidence.csv    # rule verdict and condition trail, kept separate
├── manifest.json             # provenance, fingerprints, sampling configuration
├── LABELING_GUIDE.md         # protocol and smell catalogue
└── snippets/                 # stable source snapshots for every reviewed entity
```

The reviewer enters `present`, `absent`, or `uncertain`, a human severity for
present smells, a reviewer identifier, notes, and optionally a timestamp.
`dataset validate` detects missing columns, duplicate task IDs, invalid labels,
missing reviewer IDs, and inconsistent severity values. `dataset finalize`
omits uncertain rows and writes a canonical labels-only CSV plus a SHA-256
manifest. No rule evidence or software-metric column is copied into that file.
M5 will verify the source fingerprint and recompute all features locally.

For two independent reviewers, `dataset agreement` reports exact label
agreement, Cohen's kappa over common binary decisions, severity agreement, and
optionally writes disagreements to a conflict file for adjudication.

Control sampling is deterministic: the configured seed is combined with each
project fingerprint, so rerunning M4 on unchanged source produces identical task
IDs, controls, snippets, and CSV ordering.

> **These detectors are a baseline, not ground truth.** Running them over a
> corpus and training on the output would produce a model that learns to
> reproduce the rules — near-perfect scores, flawless cross-project transfer,
> and a circular, meaningless paper. Their legitimate uses are: a non-ML
> baseline the trained model must beat; a sanity check on the metrics engine;
> a first pass for *human* labelling, where the rules propose candidates and
> people adjudicate; and coverage where no training data exists at all.

---


## M5 machine-learning and cross-project evaluation

`codesmell ml prepare` joins M4's canonical human labels to metrics recomputed
from the exact reviewed source. Project fingerprints, source-file SHA-256 values
and entity metadata are verified before rows are written. Dataset-supplied
metric columns are never trusted.

M5 trains a separate binary classifier for each smell, using either scaled
Logistic Regression or a class-balanced Random Forest. Class and method feature
spaces remain disjoint. `codesmell ml split` assigns whole projects to train or
test; no project fingerprint can appear in both partitions.

`codesmell ml train` performs an outer project holdout. Hyperparameters are
selected with grouped inner cross-validation using only the outer-training
projects, then the untouched project holdout is evaluated once. Every model
folder contains `model.joblib` and a `model_card.json` recording the exact
feature order, selected parameters, class balance, train/test projects, metrics
and model SHA-256.

`codesmell ml logo` runs leave-one-project-out evaluation. Every project becomes
the outer test project once, with grouped tuning repeated only on the remaining
projects. The report includes per-project folds, skipped-fold reasons, pooled
micro metrics and macro means. One-class held-out projects are not hidden:
ROC-AUC, PR-AUC and balanced accuracy are recorded as unavailable for those
folds while threshold-based metrics remain available.

```text
training_features.csv
training_features.csv.manifest.json
models_m5/
├── holdout_report.json
├── predictions.csv
└── <smell>/<model>/
    ├── model.joblib
    └── model_card.json
logo_m5/
├── logo_report.json
└── logo_predictions.csv
```

See `M5_EXECUTION_GUIDE.md` for the full Windows workflow.

---

## M6 FastAPI backend and persistent analysis jobs

M6 separates HTTP request handling from static analysis. The API safely stores
a `.zip` or `.py` upload, or registers an allowed public HTTPS repository, then
creates a durable database job. A separate worker atomically claims the oldest
queued job, runs the existing sandboxed ingestion, metrics and rule-detection
pipeline, and persists source metadata, entity metric vectors, findings and job
events.

SQLite is the zero-configuration local default. Alembic maintains the schema,
and `docker compose up --build` runs PostgreSQL, a one-time migration service,
the API and the worker. Worker heartbeats prevent a long analysis being claimed
twice; stale jobs are requeued until their configured attempt limit is reached.

Interactive API documentation is available at `/docs`. See
`M6_EXECUTION_GUIDE.md` for local, curl and Docker workflows. M6 intentionally
does not include authentication or public multi-tenant deployment controls.

---

## M7 trusted model serving and explainability

M7 registers M5 model folders through an administrator CLI. Registration
verifies `model.joblib` against the SHA-256 in `model_card.json`, validates the
smell, supported model kind and exact `feature__...` order, then copies the
artifact into private storage with restrictive permissions. The HTTP API does
not accept arbitrary model uploads because joblib artifacts are pickle-based
and must only be loaded from trusted, verified sources.

Analysis jobs support three modes:

- `rule`: M3 thresholds only.
- `ml`: enabled registered models only.
- `hybrid`: rule findings and ML predictions in the same job.

The worker reconstructs every vector in the exact feature order recorded by the
model card and stores the predicted class, probability, confidence and
uncertainty. Logistic Regression pipelines use SHAP `LinearExplainer` in their
standardized feature space; Random Forest models use `TreeExplainer`.
Explanations are produced in batches, and deterministic model-native
contributions remain available as a compatibility fallback.

Positive predictions receive smell-specific refactoring guidance, the strongest
metric evidence, and validation steps. Recommendations are advisory: they ask
for characterization tests and re-analysis, not metric optimization at the
expense of design.

```text
model_artifacts
ml_predictions
prediction_explanations
recommendations
```

Important endpoints:

```text
GET   /api/v1/models
PATCH /api/v1/models/{model_id}
POST  /api/v1/projects/{project_id}/analyses
GET   /api/v1/analyses/{job_id}/predictions
GET   /api/v1/analyses/{job_id}/explanations
GET   /api/v1/analyses/{job_id}/recommendations
GET   /api/v1/predictions/{prediction_id}/explanation
GET   /api/v1/predictions/{prediction_id}/recommendation
```

See `M7_EXECUTION_GUIDE.md` for the full local and Docker workflow.

