## Architecture

Hexagonal. `core/` holds the domain model and the abstract ports; everything
else is an adapter behind one of them. Nothing in `core/` performs I/O or
imports a framework.

```
src/codesmell/
├── core/                 # domain — no I/O, no frameworks, no dependencies
│   ├── enums.py          # Language, EntityType, SmellType, Severity, ...
│   ├── models.py         # frozen dataclasses: SourceFile, ProjectInventory, ...
│   ├── ports.py          # ABCs: SourceParser, MetricCalculator, SmellDetector
│   ├── registry.py       # generic plugin registry
│   └── errors.py         # typed exceptions with stable codes
├── config/
│   ├── settings.py       # pydantic-settings, env-overridable
│   └── logging.py        # structured JSON logs with job-id correlation
├── ingestion/            # M1
│   ├── sandbox.py        # per-job workspace, guaranteed teardown
│   ├── archive.py        # hardened ZIP extraction
│   ├── git.py            # validated shallow clone
│   ├── filters.py        # path/content filtering
│   ├── detection.py      # language, build tool, dependency detection
│   ├── inventory.py      # the project census
│   └── service.py        # orchestration
├── languages/            # M2
│   └── python/parser.py  # ast -> entities + language-agnostic facts
├── detectors/            # M3
│   ├── rules.py          # conditions, severity bands, exemptions
│   ├── config.py         # YAML loading, validated against the live schema
│   ├── thresholds.py     # per-project percentile distributions
│   ├── engine.py         # detection + explainable findings
│   └── default_rules.yaml
├── dataset/              # M4
│   ├── models.py         # review tasks, evidence and validation reports
│   ├── builder.py        # deterministic candidate/control sampling
│   ├── io.py             # review bundles and labels-only export
│   └── validation.py     # schema validation and reviewer agreement
├── ml/                   # M5
│   ├── features.py       # verified labels -> recomputed feature dataset
│   ├── splitting.py      # deterministic project-level holdout
│   ├── tuning.py         # grouped inner hyperparameter selection
│   ├── training.py       # per-smell models + holdout artifacts
│   ├── logo.py           # leave-one-project-out evaluation
│   ├── evaluation.py     # binary metrics and one-class handling
│   ├── inference.py      # model hash/schema verification
│   └── io.py             # strict M5 CSV schemas
├── api/                  # M6-M9 API, auth, users, reports and storage
├── auth/                 # M9 Argon2id, JWT and principals
├── db/                   # M6-M9 SQLAlchemy models and Alembic migrations
├── jobs/                 # M6-M7 queue, worker, rule/ML orchestration
├── explain/              # M7 trusted registry, SHAP and recommendations
├── reports/              # M9 JSON/CSV/HTML/PDF report generation
├── research/             # M9 holdout/LOGO tables, intervals and figures
├── metrics/              # M2
│   ├── context.py        # cross-file symbol table (the second pass)
│   ├── complexity.py     # size, cyclomatic, cognitive, nesting
│   ├── halstead.py       # Halstead suite + maintainability index
│   ├── oo.py             # CK suite, cohesion, method-level metrics
│   └── engine.py         # two-pass orchestration + FeatureSchema
├── container.py          # composition root
└── cli.py
```

The engine runs in **two passes** — parse everything, then compute — because
CBO, RFC, fan-in, DIT and NOC are undefined without a whole-project view. A
single-pass engine still produces numbers; they are just quietly wrong, with
nothing raising to say so.

Adding a language, metric, smell detector or model means writing one class
against a port in `core/ports.py` and adding one entry to a registry in
`container.py`. No downstream module changes.

---

## Security model

Uploaded archives and user-supplied URLs are treated as hostile.

| Attack | Defence |
|---|---|
| zip-slip (`../../etc/passwd`) | every member path resolved and proven inside the workspace |
| absolute-path members (`/etc/cron.d/x`, `C:/...`) | refused before extraction |
| Windows-separator traversal (`..\..\x`) | separators normalised before the check |
| symlink members | refused outright; a symlink can redirect a later write |
| special files (FIFO, socket, device) | refused by file-type bits |
| zip bombs | per-member, total-size and compression-ratio caps, enforced on **bytes actually written** rather than on the header's claim |
| member floods | member-count cap |
| SSRF via clone URL (`file://`, `ext::`, `169.254.169.254`, `localhost`) | HTTPS-only, host allowlist, no userinfo, default port only |
| credential leak / hang on private repos | `GIT_TERMINAL_PROMPT=0`, no askpass, host SSH config stripped, hard timeout |
| symlink escape during the walk | `os.walk(followlinks=False)`; symlinks recorded as rejections |
| workspace leak | context manager tears down on success *and* exception; `0o700` |

`tests/test_archive_security.py` covers each row. **Uploaded code is never
executed or imported — only read and parsed.** Registered ML artifacts are a
separate trusted-administrator input; their SHA-256 and model cards are checked
before deserialization.

Limits are settings, not constants; see `.env.example`. `git_allow_any_host`
exists for local research and is refused outright in `production`.

